
/*! \page SerialPort_dox Serial port library
 *
 * \section SerialPort_SomeTechnicalInformations Some technical informations
 *
 * \sa https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RS-232
 * \sa https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_port
 * \sa https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Serial_Programming/RS-232_Connections
 *
 * \subsection SerialPort_SomeTechnicalInformations_DteAndDce DTE and DCE
 *
 * In the world of serial communications, there are two different kinds of equipment:
 * |Abbreviation|Name                              |Examples|
 * |:----------:|:---------------------------------|:-------|
 * | DTE        |Data Terminal Equipment           |PC      |
 * | DCE        |Data Circuit-terminating Equipment|Modem   |
 * | ^          |Data Communication Equipment      |^       |
 *
 * \sa https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Serial_Programming/RS-232_Connections#Data_Terminal/Communications_Equipment
 * \sa https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_port#DTE_and_DCE
 *
 *
 * \subsection SerialPort_SomeTechnicalInformations_PinoutSignals Pinout signals
 *
 * |Abbreviation|Name               |Typical purpose                                                                       |Direction     |
 * |:----------:|:------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------:|
 * |    RTS     |Request To Send    |DTE requests the DCE prepare to transmit data                                         |DTE &rarr; DCE|
 * |    CTS     |Clear To Send      |DCE is ready to accept data from the DTE                                              |DTE &larr; DCE|
 * |    DCD     |Data Carrier Detect|DCE is receiving a carrier from a remote DCE                                          |DTE &larr; DCE|
 * |    DSR     |Data Set Ready     |DCE is ready to receive and send data                                                 |DTE &larr; DCE|
 * |    DTR     |Data Terminal Ready|DTE is ready to receive, initiate, or continue a call                                 |DTE &rarr; DCE|
 * | RI or RNG  |Ring Indicator     |DCE has detected an incoming ring signal on the telephone line                        |DTE &larr; DCE|
 * |    RTR     |Ready To Receive   |DTE is ready to receive data from DCE. If in use, RTS is assumed to be always asserted|DTE &rarr; DCE|
 *
 * \sa https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RS-232#Data_and_control_signals
 * \sa https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_port#Pinouts
 * \sa https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Serial_Programming/RS-232_Connections#Wiring_Pins_Explained
 *
 *
 * \subsection SerialPort_SomeTechnicalInformations_HWpinning HW pinning
 *
 * |9-pin|25-pin|Definition               |Direction (PC view)|
 * |:---:|:----:|:------------------------|:------------------|
 * |  1  |  8   |DCD (Data Carrier Detect)| IN                |
 * |  2  |  3   |RX (Receive Data)        | IN                |
 * |  3  |  2   |TX (Transmit Data)       | OUT               |
 * |  4  |  20  |DTR (Data Terminal Ready)| OUT               |
 * |  5  |  7   |GND (Signal Ground)      | --                |
 * |  6  |  6   |DSR (Data Set Ready)     | IN                |
 * |  7  |  4   |RTS (Request To Send)    | OUT               |
 * |  8  |  5   |CTS (Clear To Send)      | IN                |
 * |  9  |  22  |RI (Ring Indicator)      | IN                |
 *
 * \sa https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Serial_Programming/RS-232_Connections#Wiring_Pins_Explained
 * \sa https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_port#Pinouts
 *
 *
 * \subsection SerialPort_SomeTechnicalInformations_Break Break
 *
 *
 * \todo talk about break
 *
 * - A logical 0 is a positive voltage.
 * - A logical 1 is a negative voltage.
 * - The Idle state is a negative voltage (logical 1), called the MARK state.
 * - Keep TX pin at positive voltage (logical 0) is called a SPACE or BREAK.
 *
 * \todo check with a voltmeter
 *
 * \sa https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/TCSBRK.2const.html
 * \sa https://stackoverflow.com/questions/14803434/receive-read-break-condition-on-linux-serial-port
 * \sa https://wcscnet.com/tutorials/introduction-to-rs232-serial-communication/
 * \sa https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RS-232#Physical_interface
 * \sa https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qserialport.html#breakEnabled-prop
 * \sa https://e2e.ti.com/support/microcontrollers/msp-low-power-microcontrollers-group/msp430/f/msp-low-power-microcontroller-forum/152249/serial-port-break-clarification-2
 * \sa https://stackoverflow.com/questions/37540293/what-does-break-mean-in-real-terms-while-using-a-uart
 *
 *
 * \subsection SerialPort_SomeTechnicalInformations_AsciiControlCharacters ASCII control characters
 *
 * \todo talk about ASCII controls
 *
 * |Hex|Abbreviation|Unicode Control Pictures|Caret notation|C escape sequence|
 * |:-:|:----------:|:----------------------:|:------------:|:---------------:|
 * |11 | DC1 (XON)  |     U+2411             |  ^Q          |                 |
 * |13 | DC3 (XOFF) |     U+2413             |  ^S          |                 |
 *
 * \sa https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASCII#Control_code_table
 *
 *
 * \subsection SerialPort_SomeTechnicalInformations_OtherStuff Other stuff
 *
 * For data, store a TX byte + RX byte ? NO!! just a bool !
 * 
 * See bytesWritten() and readyRead()
 * 
 * Timestamp ? Not here !
 * Timestamp should be in a deticaed, like ControlAndDataSignalsLogItem
 * 
 * - ControlAndDataSignals
 * - ControlAndDataSignalsModel (not an item model.. must handle signal emission as some point in time, do not spam the UI)
 * - ControlAndDataSignalsController  better than model ? No, model is more common in Qt..
 * - ControlAndDataSignalsWidget
 * 
 * I think PinoutSignals is better (our variant also adds TX/RX)
 * - PinoutSignalsModel OR Controller ?
 *
 *
 * \section SerialPort_PinoutSignals Pinout signals
 *
 * \startuml "Pinout signals"
 * !include ClassDiagrams/SerialPortPinoutSignals.puml
 * \enduml
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignals holds the states of the various signals
 * (TX, RX, RTS, ...).
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsEventNotifier is a helper
 * that receives the various events, like QSerialPort::readyRead(),
 * QSerialPort::bytesWritten(), QSerialPort::breakEnabledChanged(),
 * QSerialPort::dataTerminalReadyChanged(), QSerialPort::requestToSendChanged().
 * It has then to get all pinout signals using QSerialPort::pinoutSignals().
 * It will then emit a signal to notify that at least some pinout signal has changed.
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsUiController is a helper to notify
 * the UI the various states it has to display.
 * As an example, if the CTS is enabled for some microseconds,
 * we have to display it ON for some minimal time, so it can be visible.
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsWidget is a UI component
 * to display the pinout signals.
 *
 * \code
 * class MainWindow : public QMainWindow
 * {
 *   Q_OBJECT
 *
 *  public:
 *
 *   explicit
 *   MainWindow(QWidget *parent = nullptr);
 *
 *  private:
 *
 *   void openPort();
 *   void closePort();
 *
 *   QSerialPort mSerialPort;
 *   Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsEventNotifier mPinoutSignalsEventNotifier;
 *   Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsUiController mPinoutSignalsUiController;
 *   Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsWidget *mPinoutSignalsWidget;
 * };
 * \endcode
 *
 * \code
 * using namespace Mdt::SerialPort;
 *
 * MainWindow::MainWindow(QWidget* parent)
 *  : QMainWindow(parent),
 *    mPinoutSignalsEventNotifier(&mSerialPort),
 *    mPinoutSignalsWidget(new PinoutSignalsWidget)
 * {
 *   connect(&mSerialPort, &QSerialPort::aboutToClose, &mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::setAboutToCloseEvent);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsEventNotifier, &PinoutSignalsEventNotifier::signalsChanged, &mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::setSignals);
 *
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::receiveDataChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setReceiveDataOn);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::transmitDataChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setTransmitDataOn);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::requestToSendChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setRequestToSendOn);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::clearToSendChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setClearToSendOn);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::dataCarrierDetectChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setDataCarrierDetectOn);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::dataSetReadyChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setDataSetReadyOn);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::dataTerminalReadyChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setDataTerminalReadyOn);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::ringIndicatorChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setRingIndicatorOn);
 *   connect(&mPinoutSignalsUiController, &PinoutSignalsUiController::breakChanged, mPinoutSignalsWidget, &PinoutSignalsWidget::setBreakOn);
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * \code
 * MainWindow::openPort()
 * {
 *   mSerialPort.open(QIODevice::ReadWrite);
 *   mPinoutSignalsEventNotifier.setPortOpen();
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * \code
 * MainWindow::closePort()
 * {
 *   mSerialPort.close();
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * For pinout signals tracing, the read buffer size of QSerialPort can be adjusted.
 * This will emit the readyRead signal more often, increasing slightly the accuracy of the RX signal:
 * \code
 * mSerialPort.setReadBufferSize(10);
 * \endcode
 *
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsLogItem ? Would have signals + timestamp... + bytes ?!
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsLogger
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsLoggerBackend ?
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsLogsGraphView ?
 *
 * Mdt::SerialPort::PinoutSignalsTrace ?
 *
 * \sa https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qserialport.html#PinoutSignal-enum
 * \sa \ref SerialPort_SomeTechnicalInformations_PinoutSignals
 *
